A box with “clear” gets clearance where its top border edge wouldn’t
otherwise be past the floats it clears. Ladybird works out that
hypothetical position from the box’s top margin, collapsed with the
margins before it and with those of its first children. It gets those
before the box is laid out, by sizing each of those boxes the way their
layout does — so a percentage margin resolves against the box it’s in,
and margins collapse through a box only where it ends up with no height.
One of those children that has “clear” itself counts for as long as
it wouldn’t get clearance where those margins put it. A float inside a
block container whose top margin the box’s margin collapses into would
move down with the box, so it’s measured from the flow position instead,
and gets the box clearance for as long as it reaches below that.

Blink keeps such floats as adjoining objects of the container, and
its HasClearancePastAdjoiningFloats() separates the margins there.
Otherwise, ApplyClearance() moves the box only where the offset that
its margin strut gives it is short of ExclusionSpace::ClearanceOffset(),
and a box whose contents change that offset is laid out again, with
LayoutResult::kBfcBlockOffsetResolved. WebKit estimates the top from
the collapsed margins, in RenderBlockFlow::estimateLogicalTopPosition(),
and clearFloatsIfNeeded() adds what computedClearDeltaForChild()
finds from there. Gecko collapses the top margin with those of the
frame’s first children ahead of its reflow, in nsBlockReflowContext::
ComputeCollapsedBStartMargin(), which makes a ReflowInput for each
of them, and nsBlockFrame::ReflowBlockFrame() asks BlockReflowState::
ClearFloats() from that position. Where a parent already counted the
margin in its own, it records the frame in mDiscoveredClearance, and the
parent is reflowed with the frame treated as having clearance.

In Chrome 157.0.8103.79, Firefox 151.0 and WebKit 26.5, the test’s 58
cases come out the same. Where a first child’s negative margin undoes
a positive margin of the clearing box itself, Chrome leaves the box 5px
lower than Firefox, WebKit and Ladybird do, which the test leaves out.
It also leaves out an empty first child that clears the same float and
has a bottom margin, where the three engines each do something else.
